_roi.png\nresults/wholebrain_fwer_clusters.png\nresults/wholebrain_t_and_fwer.png\n## codex/narps-neutral-r1-19\n?? .task-skills/\n?? analyze.py\n?? references/\n?? report.md\n?? results/\n675620fed8a134102b1b07226fe39d67630ee6f7\n675620fed8a134102b1b07226fe39d67630ee6f7\nleej3\n2026-10-16T03:15:24-04:00\nchore(study): supply materialized scientific inputs\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT\")/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- INPUT TASK ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/study-task.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- METHODS ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- ANALYSIS ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,260p' \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analyze.py\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- REPORT ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- ROOT LIST ---\\\\n'; find \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" -maxdepth 2 -mindepth 1 -print | sed \"s#$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/##\" | sort | head -250'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_WORKER_STOP_RECEIPT\")/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- INPUT TASK ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/study-task.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- METHODS ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- ANALYSIS ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,260p' \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analyze.py\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- REPORT ---\\\\n'; cat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; printf '\"'\\\\n--- ROOT LIST ---\\\\n'; find \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" -maxdepth 2 -mindepth 1 -print | sed \"s#$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/##\" | sort | head -250'","aggregated_output":"# Scientific assessment context\n\nAssess the original NARPS Hypothesis 1: a positive parametric effect of potential gain in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group.\nThe original [analysis instructions](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf) require whole-brain-corrected inference.\nThe scientific task is in `input/study-task.md`.\n\nWorkers receive 54 participant gain-effect maps from team AO86 and the corresponding cohort metadata.\nPreprocessing and participant modeling are fixed; evaluate group inference conditional on those inputs.\nJudge whether the analysis handles the intended participants, image geometry, quality, gain scaling, uncertainty and anatomical interpretation appropriately.\nThe maps represent independent participants; runs have already been combined within each person.\nEvaluate exclusions, uncertainty and multiple-comparison correction against the actual method.\nDefensible approaches can reach different conclusions; matching a prior team's answer is not the criterion.\nKeep scientific validity separate from the frozen STAMPED assessment.\nCheck how image coverage and missing observations enter the analysis; whether spatial alignment, vmPFC definition, group-model assumptions and whole-brain correction support the claimed inference; and whether quality checks, uncertainty and figures make those decisions inspectable.\nDo not require a particular mask, atlas, model or numerical result.\nParticipant variance maps are absent; judge methods against the supplied data.\n\n--- INPUT TASK ---\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\nDoes increasing potential monetary gain produce a greater fMRI response in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) in the NARPS equal-indifference group?\n\nTest the hypothesis that this response increases with potential gain.\nParticipants performed a mixed-gambles task, deciding whether to accept gambles with possible gains and losses.\nIn this group, the range of potential gains was twice the range of potential losses.\n\nInvestigate the question at the group-analysis stage using the supplied 54 participant gain-effect maps and metadata.\nPreprocessing and individual modeling are fixed inputs, described in `upstream-methods.md`; do not repeat them or download time-series scans.\nChoose scientifically defensible group models, quality checks, software and visualizations.\nAccount for what the supplied effect estimates measure and the limits of their upstream model.\nUse whole-brain correction for multiple comparisons when evaluating support for the hypothesis, as required by the original study; justify how you identify vmPFC.\n\nProduce a concise scientific report with informative figures and the code and results supporting your conclusions.\nExplain uncertainty, exclusions and limitations, including what a lack of support would mean.\nPrior teams' answers are not ground truth or a required result.\n\nSource: [NARPS original analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n\n--- METHODS ---\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n\n--- ANALYSIS ---\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Group analysis of fixed NARPS AO86 equal-indifference gain COPE maps.\n\nThe sole inferential contrast is a one-sample, positive-direction group test.\nWhole-brain family-wise error is controlled by the permutation maximum-t\ndistribution over the common map-coverage mask. No participant is excluded.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib as mpl\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport nibabel as nib\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport nilearn\nfrom nilearn import datasets, image, plotting\nimport scipy\nfrom scipy.stats import t as t_dist\nfrom scipy import ndimage\n\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nOUT = ROOT / \"results\"\nOUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nN_PERM = 5000\nSEED = 20261016\n\n\ndef sha256(path: Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef save_map(values: np.ndarray, mask: np.ndarray, ref: nib.spatialimages.SpatialImage,\n             filename: str, dtype=np.float32, outside=0) -> None:\n    full = np.full(mask.shape, outside, dtype=dtype)\n    full[mask] = values.astype(dtype)\n    hdr = ref.header.copy()\n    hdr.set_data_dtype(dtype)\n    nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(full, ref.affine, hdr), OUT / filename)\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    meta = pd.read_csv(ROOT / \"input/participants.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\")\n    sources = json.loads((ROOT / \"input/sources.json\").read_text())\n    rows = []\n    imgs, arrays = [], []\n    expected = sources[\"files\"]\n    for row in meta.itertuples(index=False):\n        rel = f\"data/{row.participant_id}_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\"\n        path = ROOT / \"input\" / rel\n        assert rel in expected, f\"No source record for {rel}\"\n        digest = sha256(path)\n        assert digest == expected[rel][\"sha256\"], f\"SHA256 mismatch: {rel}\"\n        img = nib.load(str(path))\n        arr = img.get_fdata(dtype=np.float32)\n        if imgs:\n            assert arr.shape == imgs[0].shape and np.allclose(img.affine, imgs[0].affine), f\"Geometry mismatch: {rel}\"\n        imgs.append(img)\n        arrays.append(arr)\n        rows.append({\"participant_id\": row.participant_id, \"sha256_verified\": True,\n                     \"n_voxels_zero\": int(np.sum(arr == 0)),\n                     \"n_voxels_nonfinite\": int(np.sum(~np.isfinite(arr))),\n                     \"cope_mean_nonzero\": float(arr[arr != 0].mean()),\n                     \"cope_sd_nonzero\": float(arr[arr != 0].std(ddof=1))})\n\n    Xall = np.stack(arrays)\n    assert len(meta) == 54 and meta.participant_id.is_unique\n    assert set(meta.group) == {\"equalIndifference\"}\n    assert np.isfinite(Xall).all(), \"Non-finite map values found\"\n    # Zero-valued voxels represent outside-map support; use voxels observed in\n    # every participant to ensure a common spatial denominator for max-T FWER.\n    common_support = np.all(Xall != 0, axis=0)\n    brain_img = datasets.load_mni152_brain_mask(resolution=2)\n    brain_resampled = image.resample_to_img(brain_img, imgs[0], interpolation=\"nearest\")\n    brain_mask = np.asarray(brain_resampled.dataobj) > 0\n    mask = common_support & brain_mask\n    assert mask.sum() > 0\n    X = Xall[:, mask].astype(np.float64)\n    n, v = X.shape\n    mean = X.mean(axis=0)\n    sd = X.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\n    t_obs = mean / (sd / np.sqrt(n))\n    p_unc = t_dist.sf(t_obs, n - 1)\n\n    # One-sided sign-flip permutation test. Under the null, participant effect\n    # estimates are assumed exchangeable about zero (symmetric errors).\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\n    sumsq = np.einsum(\"ij,ij->j\", X, X)\n    max_t = np.empty(N_PERM, dtype=np.float32)\n    batch = 20\n    for start in range(0, N_PERM, batch):\n        b = min(batch, N_PERM - start)\n        signs = rng.choice(np.array([-1.0, 1.0]), size=(b, n))\n        sums = signs @ X\n        # t = sum / sqrt((n*sum(x^2) - sum(x)^2)/(n-1))\n        denom = np.sqrt(np.maximum((n * sumsq[None, :] - sums * sums) / (n - 1), 1e-30))\n        max_t[start:start+b] = np.max(sums / denom, axis=1)\n    sorted_max_t = np.sort(max_t)\n    p_fwer = (1 + N_PERM - np.searchsorted(sorted_max_t, t_obs, side=\"left\")) / (N_PERM + 1)\n    cutoff = float(np.quantile(max_t, 0.95, method=\"higher\"))\n    sig = p_fwer <= 0.05\n    save_map(mean, mask, imgs[0], \"group_mean_cope.nii.gz\")\n    save_map(t_obs, mask, imgs[0], \"group_t.nii.gz\")\n    save_map(p_fwer, mask, imgs[0], \"group_p_fwer.nii.gz\", outside=np.nan)\n    save_map(sig.astype(float), mask, imgs[0], \"group_fwer05_mask.nii.gz\")\n\n    # Independent anatomical locator for vmPFC: Harvard-Oxford cortical\n    # Frontal Medial Cortex + Subcallosal Cortex (25% threshold). Resample\n    # nearest-neighbour to the supplied MNI152NLin2009cAsym map grid.\n    atlas = datasets.fetch_atlas_harvard_oxford(\"cort-maxprob-thr25-2mm\",\n                                               data_dir=ROOT / \"references/nilearn\", verbose=0)\n    labels = list(atlas.labels)\n    wanted = [labels.index(\"Frontal Medial Cortex\"), labels.index(\"Subcallosal Cortex\")]\n    atlas_data = np.asarray(atlas.maps.dataobj)\n    roi_img = nib.Nifti1Image(np.isin(atlas_data, wanted).astype(np.uint8), atlas.maps.affine)\n    roi_resampled = image.resample_to_img(roi_img, imgs[0], interpolation=\"nearest\")\n    roi = (np.asarray(roi_resampled.dataobj) > 0) & mask\n    assert roi.sum() > 0\n    roi_person = Xall[:, roi].mean(axis=1)\n    roi_mean = float(roi_person.mean())\n    roi_sd = float(roi_person.std(ddof=1))\n    roi_se = roi_sd / np.sqrt(n)\n    roi_t = roi_mean / roi_se\n    roi_p = float(t_dist.sf(roi_t, n - 1))\n    roi_ci = [float(roi_mean - t_dist.ppf(.975, n-1)*roi_se),\n              float(roi_mean + t_dist.ppf(.975, n-1)*roi_se)]\n    roi_perm = np.empty(N_PERM)\n    for i in range(N_PERM):\n        s = rng.choice(np.array([-1., 1.]), size=n)\n        roi_perm[i] = s @ roi_person / (roi_person.std(ddof=1) * np.sqrt(n))\n    roi_p_perm = float((1 + np.sum(roi_perm >= roi_t)) / (N_PERM + 1))\n    nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(roi.astype(np.uint8), imgs[0].affine, imgs[0].header),\n             OUT / \"vmPFC_proxy_roi.nii.gz\")\n\n    # Subject-level ROI distribution with mean and 95% t interval.\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.8, 4.2), constrained_layout=True)\n    jitter = rng.normal(0, .045, size=n)\n    ax.scatter(jitter, roi_person, s=24, alpha=.72, color=\"#345995\", edgecolor=\"white\", linewidth=.35)\n    ax.axhline(0, color=\"#555555\", linewidth=1, linestyle=\"--\")\n    ax.errorbar([.28], [roi_mean], yerr=[[roi_mean-roi_ci[0]], [roi_ci[1]-roi_mean]],\n                fmt=\"D\", color=\"#c3423f\", capsize=4, markersize=6, label=\"Mean and 95% CI\")\n    ax.set_xlim(-.18, .48)\n    ax.set_xticks([0, .28], [\"Participants\", \"Group mean\"])\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"Mean gain-effect COPE in vmPFC proxy (model units)\")\n    ax.set_title(\"vmPFC anatomical summary (not the whole-brain decision test)\")\n    ax.legend(frameon=False, loc=\"best\")\n    fig.savefig(OUT / \"vmPFC_roi.png\", dpi=180)\n    plt.close(fig)\n\n    # Display the positive t field for context, with whole-brain FWER\n    # significant voxels and ROI contour marked.\n    full_t = np.zeros(mask.shape, dtype=np.float32); full_t[mask] = t_obs\n    full_sig = np.zeros(mask.shape, dtype=np.uint8); full_sig[mask] = sig\n    t_img = nib.Nifti1Image(full_t, imgs[0].affine, imgs[0].header)\n    sig_img = nib.Nifti1Image(full_sig, imgs[0].affine, imgs[0].header)\n    roi_img2 = nib.Nifti1Image(roi.astype(np.uint8), imgs[0].affine, imgs[0].header)\n    positive_t = nib.Nifti1Image(np.where(full_t > 0, full_t, 0), imgs[0].affine, imgs[0].header)\n    disp = plotting.plot_stat_map(positive_t, threshold=2.0, display_mode=\"z\", cut_coords=[-10, 0, 10, 20, 30],\n                                  cmap=\"hot\", symmetric_cbar=False, colorbar=True,\n                                  title=\"Positive gain effect: uncorrected t > 2; green outline = vmPFC proxy\",\n                                  dim=-0.3, black_bg=False, annotate=True)\n    disp.add_contours(roi_img2, levels=[.5], colors=[\"#21a179\"], linewidths=1.2)\n    disp.savefig(OUT / \"wholebrain_t_and_fwer.png\", dpi=180)\n    disp.close()\n    # Separate corrected-result figure prevents the very small significant\n    # occipital clusters from being visually lost in the unthresholded field.\n    glass = plotting.plot_glass_brain(sig_img, display_mode=\"lyrz\", threshold=.5,\n                                     colorbar=False, plot_abs=False, cmap=\"Reds\",\n                                     title=\"Positive whole-brain voxelwise FWER p <= .05 (max-T permutations)\")\n    glass.savefig(OUT / \"wholebrain_fwer_clusters.png\", dpi=180)\n    glass.close()\n\n    pd.DataFrame(rows).to_csv(OUT / \"participant_qc.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\", index=False)\n    # Selected whole-brain peak/cluster summary.\n    if sig.any():\n        vals = np.where(sig, t_obs, -np.inf)\n        peak_flat = int(vals.argmax())\n        peak_ijk = np.argwhere(mask)[peak_flat]\n        peak_xyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(imgs[0].affine, peak_ijk).tolist()\n        max_sig_t = float(t_obs[peak_flat])\n        peak_pfwer = float(p_fwer[peak_flat])\n    else:\n        peak_xyz, max_sig_t, peak_pfwer = None, None, None\n    components, n_components = ndimage.label(full_sig)\n    masked_index = np.full(mask.shape, -1, dtype=np.int32)\n    masked_index[mask] = np.arange(v)\n    clusters = []\n    for label_id in range(1, n_components + 1):\n        vox_ijk = np.argwhere(components == label_id)\n        local_t = t_obs[masked_index[tuple(vox_ijk.T)]]\n        peak_ijk = vox_ijk[int(local_t.argmax())]\n        clusters.append({\"cluster_id\": label_id, \"n_voxels_6conn\": int(len(vox_ijk)),\n                         \"peak_t\": float(local_t.max()),\n                         \"peak_mni_mm\": nib.affines.apply_affine(imgs[0].affine, peak_ijk).tolist()})\n    pd.DataFrame(clusters).to_csv(OUT / \"fwer_positive_clusters.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\", index=False)\n    roi_fwer_vox = int(full_sig[roi].sum())\n    result = {\n        \"n_participants\": n, \"excluded\": [], \"n_input_maps\": len(imgs),\n        \"n_common_coverage_voxels\": int(v),\n        \"mask_rule\": \"intersection of nonzero support in every participant map and resampled MNI152 2-mm brain mask\",\n        \"permutation_method\": \"one-sample sign-flip max-t, positive one-sided, whole-brain voxelwise FWER\",\n        \"n_permutations\": N_PERM, \"seed\": SEED, \"max_t_95_cutoff\": cutoff,\n        \"n_fwer_positive_voxels\": int(sig.sum()), \"smallest_reportable_fwer_p\": 1/(N_PERM+1),\n        \"n_fwer_clusters_6conn\": len(clusters), \"n_fwer_voxels_overlapping_vmPFC_proxy\": roi_fwer_vox,\n        \"peak_sig_mni_mm\": peak_xyz, \"peak_sig_t\": max_sig_t, \"peak_sig_p_fwer\": peak_pfwer,\n        \"peak_unthresholded_t\": float(t_obs.max()),\n        \"n_uncorrected_p_lt_0_001\": int(np.sum(p_unc < .001)),\n        \"vmPFC_roi\": {\n            \"definition\": \"Harvard-Oxford cortical Frontal Medial Cortex + Subcallosal Cortex, 25% maximum-probability atlas, nearest-neighbour resampled\",\n            \"n_voxels\": int(roi.sum()), \"mean_cope\": roi_mean, \"sd_between_participants\": roi_sd,\n            \"ci95_t\": roi_ci, \"t\": float(roi_t), \"p_one_sided_parametric\": roi_p,\n            \"p_one_sided_signflip\": roi_p_perm,\n        },\n        \"software\": {\"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"nibabel\": nib.__version__,\n                     \"numpy\": np.__version__, \"pandas\": pd.__version__,\n                     \"scipy\": scipy.__version__, \"nilearn\": nilearn.__version__,\n                     \"matplotlib\": mpl.__version__},\n    }\n    (OUT / \"results.json\").write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\n--- REPORT ---\n# Potential monetary gain and fMRI response in the equal-indifference group\n\n## Finding\n\nThe supplied data do **not support a positive gain effect in vmPFC** under the prespecified whole-brain family-wise error (FWE) criterion. In the independent anatomical vmPFC proxy, the mean gain-effect estimate was 0.0027 model units (95% t CI -0.0217 to 0.0270; one-sided sign-flip *p* = .403; *n* = 54). No whole-brain FWE-positive voxel overlapped the proxy region.\n\nThe whole-brain test did find 23 positive FWE-significant voxels in three small 6-connected clusters, all in posterior occipital cortex. The largest cluster had 16 voxels, with peak *t* = 6.62 at MNI (-26, -102, -3.6) and max-*t* corrected *p* = .0010. These clusters do not support the vmPFC hypothesis. Cluster sizes are descriptive; inference was voxelwise max-*t*, not cluster-level inference.\n\n![Positive whole-brain FWE-significant voxels](results/wholebrain_fwer_clusters.png)\n\n![Uncorrected positive t field and vmPFC proxy](results/wholebrain_t_and_fwer.png)\n\n![Participant-level vmPFC estimates](results/vmPFC_roi.png)\n\n## Analysis\n\nAll 54 equal-indifference participants were included; none were excluded. Participant IDs matched the metadata, all 54 source SHA256 checksums verified, map dimensions and affines matched, and no map contained non-finite values. I fit an unweighted one-sample group *t* model to each participant's supplied gain-effect map and tested the positive direction. Whole-brain FWE was controlled at .05 with 5,000 participant-level sign-flip permutations and a maximum-*t* statistic across voxels. This preserves the observed spatial dependence while controlling the voxelwise family-wise error rate. The analysis mask was the intersection of voxels nonzero in every participant map with a resampled MNI152 2-mm brain mask (186,400 voxels). The maximum-*t* 95th percentile was 5.39. Permutation seed: 20261016.\n\nThe vmPFC locator was set without reference to the observed effect: the Harvard–Oxford cortical atlas's Frontal Medial Cortex and Subcallosal Cortex labels at the 25% maximum-probability threshold, combined as one broad anatomical proxy. The atlas labels were nearest-neighbour resampled from MNI152 to the supplied MNI152NLin2009cAsym grid. Because vmPFC has no single agreed anatomical boundary, this is a transparent proxy rather than a uniquely definitive vmPFC definition. The ROI average is a secondary descriptive check; the hypothesis decision is based on the whole-brain test, as requested in the [original NARPS instructions](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf). The atlas is described by [FSL/Harvard–Oxford](https://fsl.fmrib.ox.ac.uk/fsl/docs/other/datasets.html).\n\n## What the estimates mean and limits\n\nThese are participant contrast parameter estimates (COPEs), not *t* or *z* maps. AO86 mean-centered and variance-normalized its gain modulator before HRF convolution, so a COPE is not change in BOLD per dollar. Response-trial duration was response time, and gain was modeled alongside choice; nonresponse events and nuisance terms were also included. Gain and loss were modeled separately, so the effect is conditional on that upstream model. Since the potential-gain range was twice the potential-loss range, the coefficient scale also cannot be read as a direct gain-versus-loss sensitivity comparison.\n\nThe group *t* test treats participant estimates as independent observations and does not weight them by their first-level precision. Participant variance maps and the complete executable upstream pipeline were not supplied, so a variance-weighted mixed-effects analysis and a full upstream audit were not possible. Sign-flip inference assumes participant estimates are exchangeable and approximately symmetric under the null. The anatomical atlas and brain mask are in MNI152, whereas the COPE maps are in MNI152NLin2009cAsym; nearest-neighbour resampling is a practical approximation, and small boundary misalignments are possible.\n\nFailure to find vmPFC support means this analysis did not detect a sufficiently consistent positive group effect at whole-brain FWE .05. It is not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero: modest effects, heterogeneous responses, the chosen anatomical proxy, upstream modeling choices, and the available sample could all limit detection. NARPS itself documented substantial variation in analysis outcomes across teams ([original study](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2314-9)).\n\n## Reproducibility files\n\n- [`analyze.py`](analyze.py): checksum and geometry checks, group inference, anatomical ROI summary, figures and results.\n- [`results/results.json`](results/results.json): machine-readable summary.\n- [`results/participant_qc.tsv`](results/participant_qc.tsv): participant-level input checks and descriptive values.\n- [`results/fwer_positive_clusters.tsv`](results/fwer_positive_clusters.tsv): descriptive connected components among FWE-positive voxels.\n- `results/group_*.nii.gz`: mean, *t*, corrected *p*, and positive FWE mask.\n- `results/vmPFC_proxy_roi.nii.gz`: resampled anatomical proxy.\n\nRun from this directory with the supplied Python environment:\n\n```bash\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/common-environment/.pixi/envs/default/bin/python analyze.py\n```\n\n--- ROOT LIST ---\n.codex\n.codex/hooks.json\n.entire\n.entire/.gitignore\n.entire/logs\n.entire/metadata\n.entire/settings.local.json\n.entire/tmp\n.git\n.git/annex\n.git/COMMIT_EDITMSG\n.git/config\n.git/entire-redact-cache\n.git/entire-session-locks\n.git/entire-sessions\n.git/entire-shadow-locks\n.git/HEAD\n.git/hooks\n.git/index\n.git/info\n.git/logs\n.git/objects\n.git/refs\n.task-skills\n.task-skills/jupyter-notebook\n.task-skills/pdf\nanalyze.py\ninput\ninput/data\ninput/participants.tsv\ninput/sources.json\ninput/study-task.md\ninput/upstream-methods.md\noutput\nreferences\nreferences/nilearn\nreport.md\nresults\nresults/fwer_positive_clusters.tsv\nresults/group_fwer05_mask.nii.gz\nresults/group_mean_cope.nii.gz\nresults/group_p_fwer.nii.gz\nresults/group_t.nii.gz\nresults/participant_qc.tsv\nresults/results.json\nresults/run.log\nresults/vmPFC_proxy_roi.nii.gz\nresults/vmPFC_roi.png\nresults/wholebrain_fwer_clusters.png\nresults/wholebrain_t_and_fwer.png\ntmp\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
